<!--
    An executable's own `main` gets the C runtime's declaration, TRUNCATED to
    the arguments the body reads (`option elfmain on|off`, default on).

    The XML wrapper only provides the harness architecture; every pass loads an
    in-tree ELF through the CLI's own loader. `elfmainnamed_x86_64` is a gcc -O2
    PIE whose `main` reads `argc` and `argv[1]` and never `envp` (source beside
    it); `elfmainvoid_x86_64` is a gcc -O2 PIE whose `main` reads no argument at
    all and calls `tick()`.

    Pass 1 (`elfmain off`, named) reproduces the defect: the symbol names the
    function `main`, but its declaration is whatever its body alone yields:
    an unsigned return plus two anonymous slots, the second a struct whose
    fields are `argv[0]` and `argv[1]`.

    Pass 2 (the default, named) applies the C runtime's declaration, truncated
    to the two arguments this body reads: `int main(int argc, char **argv)`,
    with `argv[1]` read as an element of the argument vector. The locked prototype
    is exactly the arity the body reads, so no third parameter is invented.

    Passes 3 and 4 are the `elfmainvoid` fixture, whose `main` reads nothing.
    Off, the body alone gives `main(void)` returning a guessed width; the
    default names it `int main(void)`, and (the point of the truncation) the
    call to `tick()` still takes no argument. Declaring argc/argv/envp here would
    have handed `tick` a phantom one.

    Passes 5 and 6 are the shapes the declaration must leave exactly as the
    analysis finds them (`elfmainfwd_x86_64.c`, built twice). In
    `elfmainfwd_x86_64` main hands argc and argv unchanged to its first call,
    so the analysis recovers them as whole 64-bit registers read only by that
    call; a 32-bit argc would leave half of that argument unassigned, so main
    keeps both parameters and `count(a0,a1)`. In `elfmainargv_x86_64` main reads
    only argv, after a call to `ping()`; the analysis recovers an unread slot
    ahead of it, and declaring that slot argc would make it a live input, so
    main keeps that form and the call to `ping()` gains nothing.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="harness_stub"/>
</binaryimage>
